   "translation": {
    "literal": "To the twice-born and the gods who dwell from Brahmā’s world to Śeṣa, to the Lokāloka mountain — to them always homage, homage. Let the beings move away, the beings settled on the earth; the beings that make obstacles, let them go by Śiva’s command. The fierce spirits, piśācas and the rest, who are a burden on the earth — without their opposition I begin the Vedic rite. Earth, by you the worlds are held; goddess, you are held by Viṣṇu; and you, goddess, hold me, and make the seat pure.",
    "readable": "Homage always to the twice-born and the gods who live anywhere from Brahmā’s heaven down to Śeṣa and out to the mountain at the world’s rim. Let the spirits that dwell on the earth, the makers of obstacles, go away at Śiva’s command; let the fierce ones and the piśācas not stand in the way as I begin this Vedic rite. Earth, you hold up the worlds and Viṣṇu holds you up: hold me too, goddess, and make my seat pure."
   }
  }
 ],
 "meaning": "Four traditional verses of the prayoga, said before sitting for the Gāyatrī japa. The first greets every twice-born being and god in the whole extent of the world, from Brahmā’s heaven to Śeṣa below and the Lokāloka range at the edge; the second and third ask the spirits that cause obstacles to withdraw, so that the Vedic rite can begin unopposed; the fourth turns to the Earth, who holds the worlds and is held by Viṣṇu, and asks her to hold the worshipper and purify the seat.",
 "in_the_rite": "Step āsana, before the japa, all three services, in the Mylapore prayoga only (S lacks the step).",
 "notes": [
  {
   "label": "linguistic",
   "text": "Four anuṣṭubh (śloka) verses. Only M has them."
  }
 ],
